  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+7y.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{\u23df}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}.\n$$",
